[Music]  you  hi I'm will Torres and I'm joined by my  colleague Tiffany Baroni Manolo we're  going to be talking about financial aid  here at Stanford GSB and we're gonna be  going over some frequently answered  questions in today's conversation we're  going to talk about who should apply for  financial aid when you should apply and  the types of aid available to you after  we go through the basics we're gonna  bring in some current students who went  through the financial aid process and  will be sharing their experiences there  are two terms - no need blind and  need-based the admission process is  need-blind which means we're not taking  into account how you're gonna pay for  your education when we're making those  admissions decisions the financial aid  process happens after you're admitted  and financial aid here at Stanford is  need-based that means you're gonna be  working with our financial aid officers  to take a look at your financial  circumstances to figure out a financial  aid package that will support you during  your time here the financial aid process  is centered around our schools guiding  principles of access equity and  excellence we want every student who is  admitted to be able to engage and  participate to the fullest extent during  their time here to do this we've  developed a financial aid process to  treat students consistently and  equitably while supporting the GSB s  efforts to attract the most competitive  and diverse class possible we understand  that a Stanford degree is an investment  in you and your future and we hope that  with our financial aid support you'll be  able to make that investment with the  goal of supporting those who need it the  most with the most aid so I'm going to  provide you with a general overview of  financial aid at Stanford GSB so all  admitted students into the MBA and MSX  program are eligible to apply for  need-based fellowship and loans loans  are specific based off of your  citizenship we determine your cost of  attendance based off of your unique  financial situation so marital status as  well as family size your cost of  attendance takes into consideration your  tuition living expenses housing charges  health insurance etc we determine your  student contribution based off of the  information reported in your application  we take into consideration special  circumstances which would include  medical expenses  support to family as well as your family  size on average 50% of our students  receive a need-based fellowship which  equates to about 76 thousand dollars for  the two-year MBA program and $50,000 for  the one year MSX program the GSB has an  expectation that all students will  contribute a portion of their income and  assets towards their educational costs  one of the most commonly question asked  questions that I get is how do you  determine a student's fellowship  eligibility yes that is a typical  question that we received from  perspective admitted students so in  order to determine somebody's need-based  fellowship eligibility we're going to  look at their income and assets to  determine their student contribution  which is what they should be able to  contribute towards their education we  take that student contribution plus a  base loan and deduct it from their costs  of attendance to determine what their  financial need is of that financial need  we're going to give as much as we can in  need-based fellowship and the rest would  come in the form of loans which are  specific based off your citizenship and  just coming from lived experience life  is complicated so how do you take into  account personal and/or like unique  circumstances from applicants yeah  that's a great question so within our  financial aid application we have a  section specifically for students to  call outs with unique financial  situations and provide us contacts and  we will review them and take them into  consideration on a case-by-case basis  all right and then what about folks who  are thinking about nonprofit or public  sector areas historically that might  have a different pay trajectory yeah so  what's really amazing is that the GSB  offers a loan forgiveness program for  both the mba and MSX alumni who are  working in a non-profit public service  sector great and how do you consider  folks coming from outside of the United  States so international versus US  citizens  yeah so for us for financial aid  purposes we treat both internationals  and USS and permanent residents exactly  the same in our financial aid algorithm  to determine need-based aid eligibility  so whatever one student will get will be  the same that somebody else would get in  the calculation right and then what if  I'm coming to the GSB with a spouse or  children how do you take that into  account yeah so if you're coming with a  spouse or children we're gonna use a  higher cost of attendance because you  have higher expenses when we're  determining your student your student  contribution as well as your fellowship  eligibility in addition Stanford  University as a whole offers a family  grant program that will offer further  assistance to individuals who are coming  with dependent children yeah  and let's say I admit I'm admitted  I'm applying for financial aid and that  supports really important to how I'm  deciding where I'm gonna go are there  any tools or places you could point me  to to help at least ballpark what I  might get yes so that's a really good  question and we actually have a  financial aid calculator available on  the my GSP website once you're admitted  into the program where you can put in  your information that we would ask for  in your financial aid application and  get a range for what your student  contribution would be as well as your  need-based fellowship eligibility so  that would help you get some informed  decisions about whether or not you want  to go through the process yeah and then  final question do you have any tips best  practices or things I should consider as  I'm thinking about funding my b-school  education so one of the first things is  we say start with the website because  all the contents available there for you  to understand how to apply for aid what  aids available to you and a leader type  in nuances and then always reach out to  our office because we're here to be your  help to be helpful to you to answer any  questions to get you through the process  and it's a conversation so don't be  afraid to tell us about a special  circumstance that's going on in your  life because it might be something we  can take into consideration thank you  and so with that we invite you to learn  more about financial aid at for both the  NBA and the MSX programs log on GSB dot  stanford.edu to learn more  [Music]

